WEBVTT

q0001
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.292
What had to be hidden to make one method look fastest everywhere?

q0002
00:00:06.292 --> 00:00:15.411
This episode has one job: A single podium destroys the output, workload, boundary, preparation, reuse, and uncertainty information needed to interpret method performance.

q0003
00:00:15.411 --> 00:00:24.530
We build on Why scopes and boundaries matter, Corrected B2/B4 V3 kernel result, EPFL AND/INV parity and its mechanism; their definitions stay fixed while this lesson adds a new layer.

q0004
00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:33.254
A fastest-method ranking is meaningful only for one matched output, workload, boundary, reuse pattern, and comparator set.

q0005
00:00:33.254 --> 00:00:41.005
Watch for the label blended ranking; it stays attached to the artifact this episode means.

q0006
00:00:41.005 --> 00:00:48.270
When we say task-matched comparison, the highlight identifies its layer before we interpret it.

q0007
00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:56.994
The term decision grid receives its own visual state so it cannot drift into a neighboring concept.

q0008
00:00:56.994 --> 00:01:06.113
The next sequence uses Small AND/INV cone as a conceptual teaching example, not as measured benchmark evidence.

q0009
00:01:06.113 --> 00:01:10.460
Our stable example is Small AND/INV cone.

q0010
00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:19.579
A conceptual five-input AND/INV cone whose output has four-variable semantic support, paired later with separately labeled retained EPFL evidence.

q0011
00:01:19.579 --> 00:01:25.871
Teach cone support, fanout, lowering, exact digests, and the accepted EPFL mechanism.

q0012
00:01:25.871 --> 00:01:33.622
We now construct the Small AND/INV cone and name every part before interpreting it.

q0013
00:01:33.622 --> 00:01:38.455
Construct a tempting fastest-method podium by hiding scope labels.

q0014
00:01:38.455 --> 00:01:46.693
Restore output contract, workload, boundary, reuse, and uncertainty until the podium becomes a conditional decision grid.

q0015
00:01:46.693 --> 00:01:51.526
Now isolate anti-podium argument inside Small AND/INV cone.

q0016
00:01:51.526 --> 00:01:59.764
The example definition and output meaning stay fixed while only the state for anti-podium argument changes.

q0017
00:01:59.764 --> 00:02:08.002
That matched before-and-after view assigns the visible consequence to anti-podium argument, not to a substituted workload.

q0018
00:02:08.002 --> 00:02:14.294
A glossy three-place podium appears with method logos but no task labels.

q0019
00:02:14.294 --> 00:02:20.586
Current evidence does not support a universal claim that CM beats CSE-flat.

q0020
00:02:20.586 --> 00:02:25.419
Read that statement only within this scope: cross-study synthesis.

q0021
00:02:25.419 --> 00:02:30.252
Its declared measurement boundary is multiple boundaries kept separate.

q0022
00:02:30.252 --> 00:02:35.085
The uncertainty field says scopes are intentionally not pooled.

q0023
00:02:35.085 --> 00:02:44.204
Every ratio must state numerator, denominator, workload, measurement boundary, and uncertainty; below one favors CM only for CM/comparator.

q0024
00:02:44.204 --> 00:02:49.524
Read that statement only within this scope: video evidence contract.

q0025
00:02:49.524 --> 00:02:53.871
Its declared measurement boundary is all numeric scenes.

q0026
00:02:53.871 --> 00:02:57.732
The uncertainty field says declared per ratio.

q0027
00:02:57.732 --> 00:03:06.851
CM, CM-IR, SAT, BDD, minimization, and symbolic algebra answer different questions and must be compared under matched output contracts.

q0028
00:03:06.851 --> 00:03:13.143
Read that statement only within this scope: implemented backend and task contracts.

q0029
00:03:13.143 --> 00:03:17.004
Its declared measurement boundary is output contract.

q0030
00:03:17.004 --> 00:03:20.378
The uncertainty field says scope dependent.

q0031
00:03:20.378 --> 00:03:29.497
The nearest confusing lesson is Truth digests, alternating schedules, clustering, and intervals; it owns truth-digest exactness, while this episode owns anti-podium argument.

q0032
00:03:29.497 --> 00:03:37.248
The cone starts from five named inputs, but the displayed output has four-variable semantic support.

q0033
00:03:37.248 --> 00:03:42.568
Holding that element fixed lets this episode isolate anti-podium argument.

q0034
00:03:42.568 --> 00:03:49.833
The inert input stays outlined so nominal width cannot be mistaken for active support.

q0035
00:03:49.833 --> 00:03:57.584
Structural counts describe nodes, edges, and operations; they do not directly measure hardware memory traffic.

q0036
00:03:57.584 --> 00:04:06.308
The conceptual mechanism and EPFL evidence use matched colors but retain distinct status badges and source footers.

q0037
00:04:06.308 --> 00:04:14.059
Current CM evidence deliberately contains a kernel advantage, a parity workload, and a slower wrapper.

q0038
00:04:14.059 --> 00:04:19.865
Name the missing label that invalidates each of three apparent rankings.

q0039
00:04:19.865 --> 00:04:25.671
The honest answer is conditional because the actual tasks are different.

q0040
00:04:25.671 --> 00:04:31.477
Now isolate task/boundary decision grid inside Small AND/INV cone.

q0041
